= Current Test: Vendor Pricing =

== Scope ==
Using [https://www.eqtraders.com/articles/article_page.php?article=g190&menust EQ Traders Vendor Pricing] as a reliable'ish source, we can assume the following:

* Charisma has meaningful breakpoints, especially between ~61-76 and ~100+, after which additional CHA has diminishing or no effect.
* Faction breaks into three functional tiers for pricing:
# Apprehensive, Indifferent or better, and Dubious or worse. (Indifferent-or-better vendors give identical prices for the same charisma--Ally does not improve prices beyond Indifferent.)
# Dubious-or-worse vendors won’t sell to you at all.
# Apprehensive vendors will sell, but prices are significantly worse unless charisma is very high.

These two facts define the scope of our current testing: you only need to test Apprehensive and Indifferent factions across a range of charisma values.

== Control Variables ==

'''Vendor''': Use one vendor for the entire test.

'''Item''': Use one item with a stable base price (e.g., Water Flask, Bandage, but the higher priced the better).

'''Faction level''': You must be able to reliably set your faction to:

* Apprehensive
* Indifferent
* (Optional) Amiable / Kindly / Warmly / Ally -- but these will all give identical prices.

'''Charisma value''': You need a reproducible way to set CHA to specific values (e.g., 50, 75, 85, 100, 120, 125, 150, 175, 200) -- (recommend crafted CHA jewelry and farming some CHA armor)

